  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2023 article presents an early but clear uptake signal for SciScore as a tool for improving reproducibility and reporting rigor. The work has 3 citations, 5 documented downstream reuse instances, and has already been cited in journals spanning reproducibility metrics, open science policy, and academic publishing. Its record is still modest, but the citation pattern suggests the article is being used in method- and policy-oriented discussions rather than remaining isolated to its own niche.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Assessing the Effectiveness of SciScore in Supporting the Reproducibility of Scientific Research argues that rigor and transparency are essential for research that can be replicated and reproduced, and it evaluates SciScore as a manuscript screening tool against consensus recommendations from journals and funders. That framing places the paper squarely within current reproducibility and research-integrity debates, and the bibliographic record shows that the article has begun to circulate in exactly those communities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is selective but meaningful downstream use. The article has been cited 3 times in OpenAlex, with citations appearing in 2025 and 2026. Those citing works are themselves aligned with open science, reproducibility measurement, and scholarly publishing practice, including Royal Society Open Science and JAMA Network Open. This pattern suggests the paper is being treated as a relevant contribution to ongoing conversations about how to evaluate and strengthen research transparency.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse evidence points in the same direction. The record shows 5 documented downstream reuse instances, including two Europe PMC article mentions and three article-level reuse events overall. While this is not a large volume, it is a useful early sign that the work has been incorporated into related scholarly discussion beyond the original publication venue. The reuse and citation profiles are consistent with a paper that is being used as a reference point for reproducibility infrastructure rather than as a broad public-facing result.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article also sits within a funded research program supported by NIH-related awards across multiple institutes, indicating that it is part of a broader stewardship effort focused on research quality and reproducibility. Combined with its open-access status and indexing in biomedical and repository systems, the paper is positioned for continued reuse as the field develops. Overall, the record shows early but credible scholarly uptake, especially in literature concerned with open science, reporting standards, and reproducibility tools.</p>",
